\description{
The book's backtest engine. Every strategy in \code{strategies} is fitted on the
same rolling estimation windows, over the same universe, on the same
rebalancing calendar, and reported on the same set of metrics — gross of
trading costs and net of them, side by side, never blended. Each strategy is
also passed through \code{\link[=diagnose_rebalancing]{diagnose_rebalancing()}}, because
\code{\link[PortfolioAnalytics:optimize.portfolio.rebalancing]{PortfolioAnalytics::optimize.portfolio.rebalancing()}} does not report a
solver failure and a backtest that quietly held a vector of \code{NA}s for a fifth
of its life looks exactly like one that did not.
}
\section{The off-by-one in \code{rolling_window}}{

\code{optimize.portfolio.rebalancing()} estimates period \code{ep} on
\code{R[(ep - rolling_window):ep, ]}, which is \code{rolling_window + 1} observations,
not \code{rolling_window}. Ask for a 60-month window and you get 61 months of data.
\code{pm_backtest()} therefore passes \code{rolling_window = estimation_window - 1} and
\code{training_period = estimation_window}, so that \code{estimation_window = 60} really
does mean sixty observations in every window including the first. If you call
\code{optimize.portfolio.rebalancing()} directly, do the subtraction yourself.
}

\section{Why \code{full_investment} has to be widened by a millionth}{

\code{PortfolioAnalytics:::gmv_opt()} writes \code{sum(w) = 1} as \strong{two opposing
inequalities}, \code{sum(w) >= min_sum} and \code{-sum(w) >= -max_sum}, and hands them
to \code{quadprog} through \code{ROI}. When \code{min_sum == max_sum} those two rows are
active at the same point, and \code{quadprog}'s dual active-set method resolves the
degeneracy by declaring the problem infeasible: \strong{40 of the 313 minimum-
variance windows} over the 16 risky class series come back with
\code{"constraints are inconsistent, no solution!"} for a problem whose entire
constraint set is \code{sum(w) = 1} and \verb{0 <= w <= 1}.

It is not a conditioning problem. Those covariance matrices have condition
numbers around \code{1.6e4} and the same windows solve instantly once the pair is
separated. It is not a scaling problem either: putting the returns in percent
only moves the count from 40 to 25. Dropping either the redundant row or the
vacuous zero equality row that \code{gmv_opt()} also emits when there is no return
objective takes it to zero, and so does separating \code{min_sum} from \code{max_sum} by
any positive amount.

\code{ROI} reports the failure faithfully in \code{result$status$code};
\code{PortfolioAnalytics:::gmv_opt()} reads \code{result$solution} without looking at
the status, so the period comes back with weights of \code{NA} and no complaint.
That is issue #45, and \code{\link[=diagnose_rebalancing]{diagnose_rebalancing()}} catches it as criterion 2.

\code{sum_tolerance} is the fix: \code{min_sum} becomes \code{1 - 1e-6} and \code{max_sum} becomes
\code{1 + 1e-6}, all 313 windows solve, and the solutions sit at the lower bound
with \code{sum(w) = 0.999999}, a millionth of the book in cash. The
\code{PortfolioAnalytics} documentation suggests \code{0.99} and \code{1.01} for the same
purpose; that leaves the solver a full percent of slack, which it takes, and
one percent of a portfolio is not a rounding error.
}

\section{What is parallel, and what only looks as though it is}{

\code{optimize.portfolio.rebalancing()} already contains a
\verb{foreach(...) \%dopar\%} over the rebalancing dates, so the periods are
embarrassingly parallel and the machinery to run them that way is in the
package. What is missing is the other half: \code{optimize.portfolio()} registers a
backend in its own body, and \code{optimize.portfolio.rebalancing()} does not. With
no backend registered, \verb{\%dopar\%} silently falls back to sequential execution
and \code{foreach} says so once, in a warning about "no parallel backend
registered", which is easy to read as noise. A reader can therefore run a
313-period backtest on a 14-core machine, on one core, and never notice.

\code{workers} closes that half. It is not
\code{\link[PortfolioAnalytics:optimize.portfolio.parallel]{PortfolioAnalytics::optimize.portfolio.parallel()}}, which is a different
function for a different job: its \code{nodes} argument runs the \emph{same} single
optimisation repeatedly, which is what a stochastic solver or a random
portfolio search needs. It does nothing for a rolling backtest.

Two consequences of running on workers, both worth knowing before debugging a
backtest:
\itemize{
\item A period whose solver \strong{throws} still comes back, because
\code{.errorhandling = "pass"} returns the condition object as that period's
result. \code{\link[=diagnose_rebalancing]{diagnose_rebalancing()}} catches it as criterion 1 either way.
\item A period whose solver \strong{warns or messages} loses that output entirely: it
was emitted in the worker process and nothing collects it. Diagnose a
suspicious backtest sequentially, then re-run it in parallel.
}

Strategies declared with a \code{weight_fun} do not go through
\code{optimize.portfolio.rebalancing()} at all and are evaluated sequentially.
They are rules, not searches, and take milliseconds.
}

\section{Timing, and why there is no look-ahead}{

The window for a rebalancing date \code{d} ends \strong{at} \code{d}; the weights it produces
are applied to the return of the \emph{following} period. Nothing estimated at \code{d}
touches a return earned on or before \code{d}. With monthly data, a 60-month window
and 372 months, the first rebalancing date is month 60 and the first
out-of-sample return is month 61.
}

\section{What happens when a period does not solve}{

The weights of a failed period cannot be traded on, so the previous period's
weights are carried forward and a warning names how many periods that
happened in. If the \emph{first} rebalancing date fails there is nothing to carry
and the function aborts. The count is reported in the \code{n_failed} column
whatever happens: a backtest with carried-forward weights is a different
strategy from the one that was specified, and the table has to say so.
}

\section{Gaps}{

A missing return is an error, not something to impute. \code{PortfolioAnalytics}
will not warn about one, but it silently changes the effective sample of every
moment estimate and, in the drift accounting, makes a portfolio return
undefined. Fill or drop before the boundary, not after.
}

\section{Costs, turnover and the two bases}{

Turnover in period \code{t} is \code{0.5 * sum(abs(w_bop - w_eop_previous))}, the
standard one-way fraction of the book, including the initial purchase in the
first period. \code{ann_turnover} is its mean times \code{periods_per_year}. The cost
charged is \code{sum(cost_i * abs(w_bop - w_eop_previous))}, per asset and not
flat, subtracted from that period's return to give the \code{"net"} basis. Between
rebalancing dates the weights drift and nothing is traded, so nothing is
charged.
}

